  "final_answer": "**Search set up:** One-way, Dublin (DUB) → Athens (ATH), 1 adult, departing Wednesday 30 December 2026, prices in EUR — Google Flights page \"Dublin to Athens\".\n\n**Flights on Wed, Dec 30 (cheapest first)**\n- **€109 — Ryanair, 5:45 AM → 11:50 AM, nonstop, 4 hr 5 min** (fare excludes overhead-bin/carry-on access; add the Bags filter to see bag-inclusive prices). This is the best value by a wide margin.\n- €227 — British Airways, 5:50 PM → 3:20 PM+1, 1 stop LHR, 19 hr 30 min (14 hr 20 min layover).\n- €256 — Turkish Airlines, 10:40 AM → 8:40 AM+1, 1 stop IST, 20 hr (14 hr layover).\n- €276 — Turkish Airlines, 10:40 AM → 9:00 PM, 1 stop IST, 8 hr 20 min.\n- €331 — Turkish Airlines + Aegean, 10:40 AM → 10:30 PM, 1 stop IST, 9 hr 50 min.\n- €393 — Lufthansa/Aegean, 5:10 AM → 1:40 PM, 1 stop FRA, 6 hr 30 min.\n- €414 — KLM, 8:50 AM → 4:30 PM, 1 stop AMS, 5 hr 40 min.\n- €442 — British Airways, 10:30 AM → 7:15 PM, 1 stop LHR, 6 hr 45 min.\n\n**Price graph analysis (next 2 months)**\nGoogle's own verdict on the €109 fare: *\"Prices are currently typical for your search\"* — the least expensive similar trips to Athens usually run **€70–235**, and €109 sits comfortably inside that band, so Dec 30 is a fair, not inflated, price for the holiday period.\n\nThe price graph (one-way, anchored on Wed, Dec 30 at €109) plots daily fares from late December through **January 2027 and February 2027** on a €0–€300+ scale. The shape is a low, mostly flat trough: the great majority of January and February bars sit in the **roughly €70–130 band**, with a handful of taller spikes reaching toward **€200–€250** around specific dates, and nothing approaching the €300+ top of the axis. In other words, the two months after your date are consistently cheap — the €109 you'd pay on Dec 30 is at or slightly above the floor of that window, and shifting the trip a few days into January would likely land you nearer the €70–90 low end.\n\nOne caveat on precision: the graph bars are rendered graphics rather than text, so I could confirm the axis, the date range, the €109 anchor and the overall band/spike pattern, but I could not extract an exact euro figure for every individual day in January–February. The date grid did confirm the seasonal low point clearly: **Monday, November 16, 2026 at €39** is flagged as the \"Cheapest price\" in the surrounding window — well below anything in the Dec–Feb range.\n\n**Bottom line:** Book the €109 Ryanair nonstop for Dec 30 if the date is fixed — it's a typical, reasonable fare and by far the fastest option (4 hr nonstop vs 5.5–20 hr with connections). If the date is flexible, the graph shows January–February as a broad cheap trough where you'd likely save €20–40, and mid-November at €39 is the outright bargain. Remember the Ryanair fare excludes cabin bag access, so budget extra if you're travelling with more than a personal item.",
